{
 "lead": "The liquid rate in an absorber cannot be made as small as you like. Lower it and the operating line swings down towards equilibrium; the driving force shrinks, and at one rate the line just touches the curve. That touch is the pinch, and the rate there is (L/G)_min, the smallest solvent flow that still reaches the required Y_out. Design starts by finding it; working columns run at 1.2 to 2 times this value.",
 "prereq": "You need the operating line and the equilibrium line on the Y–X plot from <a href=\"#equilibrium-and-operating-lines-in-absorption\">Chapter 3.2</a>, and solubility from <a href=\"#gas-solubility-in-liquids-and-henrys-law\">Chapter 3.1</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Why the operating line stops falling",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The operating line is a solute balance: Y = Y_out + (L/G)(X − X_in). Y is kmol solute per kmol carrier gas, X is kmol solute per kmol solvent, L and G are solvent and carrier-gas flows in kmol/h, so L/G is the slope. Lean gas leaves at the top where fresh solvent enters, so the top point (X_in, Y_out) is fixed. Changing L/G rotates the line about that point."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The vertical gap between the operating and equilibrium lines is the driving force. At exactly the minimum slope the gap falls to zero at one point — the pinch — so the column needs infinite stages. Any flatter and the operating line crosses equilibrium: the driving force goes negative and the separation is impossible at any height. With a straight or convex equilibrium line the pinch sits at the rich-gas bottom, where X is largest; a strongly concave curve can pinch in the middle, so check every X."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Construction and formula",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "steps",
     "items": [
      "Draw Y* against X from solubility data.",
      "Mark the fixed top point (X_in, Y_out) and the horizontal Y = Y_in.",
      "Where that horizontal meets the curve is the pinch (X*_in, Y_in): the liquid in equilibrium with the entering gas.",
      "Join the top point to the pinch; that line's slope is (L/G)_min."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "(L/G)_min = (Y_in − Y_out) / (X*_in − X_in)",
     "where": "Y_in, Y_out are solute ratios in the entering and leaving gas (kmol solute/kmol carrier gas); X_in is the ratio in the entering solvent; X*_in is the liquid ratio in equilibrium with Y_in. All four are mole ratios."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For fresh solvent (X_in = 0) and a straight line Y* = mX, X*_in = Y_in/m, so (L/G)_min = m(1 − Y_out/Y_in). With a curved line, (Y − Y_out)/(X* − X_in) is the ratio needed at each X; the largest value is the minimum."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Choosing the design rate",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Design uses 1.2 to 2 times the minimum, most often 1.2 to 1.5. Below 1.2 the column becomes very tall; above 2 the height saved is small but pumping keeps costing."
    },
    {
     "type": "bullets",
     "items": [
      "A larger L/G raises the driving force, so the column is shorter: fewer stages in <a href=\"#tray-absorbers-theoretical-stages-and-absorption-factor\">Chapter 3.5</a>, less packed height in <a href=\"#htu-ntu-and-design-of-packed-absorbers\">Chapter 3.7</a>.",
      "It means more liquid pumped, bigger pipes and a wider column.",
      "It gives a weaker outlet liquid (smaller X_out), which costs more to regenerate in <a href=\"#non-isothermal-absorption-and-stripping\">Chapter 3.8</a>."
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
