 "beyond": "Not for the exam. A good solvent keeps the equilibrium line shallow — small m, high solubility for the solute. Then X*_in = Y_in/m is large and (L/G)_min is small. Amine solutions absorb CO2 far more strongly than water, so their effective m is much lower, and that is why they beat water. The upper limit on liquid rate is flooding, so the window between 1.2 times minimum and the flood point can be narrow (see <a href=\"#packed-towers-tower-packings-and-packing-characteristics\">Chapter 3.6</a>). Design software treats the ratio as a costing variable and minimises total annual cost."
